Package: sleeve
Version: @VERSION@
Architecture: @ARCH@
Maintainer: Matthew Hagrelius <matthew@hagreli.us>
Installed-Size: @INSTALLED_SIZE@
Depends: @DEPENDS@
Section: sound
Priority: optional
Homepage: https://github.com/mhagrelius/sleeve
Description: Find out where to buy an album
 Sleeve answers one question: given an album, where should the money go? It is
 not a "where can I stream this" tool. It ranks the ways of getting a record
 best-first and shows the arithmetic behind every placement.
 .
 Four principles decide the order, and the first two are guaranteed by the sort
 rather than by the weights: owning DRM-free files beats renting, higher artist
 payout beats lower, master quality beats format specs, and what actually exists
 beats what would be ideal. Bit depth and sample rate are tiebreakers only, so a
 24-bit/192 kHz stream can never outrank a 16-bit/44.1 kHz purchase.
 .
 Search is a first-class part of it, because album and artist names collide
 constantly — soundtracks and classical worst of all. Sleeve returns a ranked
 list of candidates rather than guessing, keeps similarly-named records in a
 separate section, and lets you drill from an album to a specific pressing,
 since the original and the remaster are different products with different
 masters.
 .
 It consults MusicBrainz, Discogs, Bandcamp, Qobuz, the iTunes Store, Odesli and
 the Dynamic Range Database. Two of those need credentials, set in
 ~/.config/sleeve/config.toml; a source that is unconfigured or unreachable
 leaves a visible "not checked" row rather than quietly shrinking the answer.
 .
 Scoring weights, locale and currency are all configurable in the same file.
